5, 0.0265655517578125, -0.0072479248046875, -0.006404876708984375, 0.0611572265625, 0.0102691650390625, 0.034820556640625, -0.037933349609375, 0.00125885009765625, -0.01488494873046875, 0.0090789794921875, 0.00762939453125, -0.04107666015625, 0.007106781005859375, -0.0311126708984375, 0.00850677490234375, -0.01898193359375, 0.0006551742553710938, 0.006572723388671875, -0.032623291015625, 0.0037078857421875, -0.0066375732421875, -0.021697998046875, -0.01441192626953125, 0.0310821533203125, 0.005126953125, 0.0156707763671875, -0.0022735595703125, 0.01120758056640625, -0.004543304443359375, -0.0107879638671875, -0.01189422607421875, -0.0235595703125, 0.01082611083984375, 0.057647705078125, 0.01039886474609375, 0.01282501220703125, 0.00771331787109375, -0.0270538330078125, 0.0140228271484375, 0.00356292724609375, 0.00257110595703125, -0.022613525390625, 0.0340576171875, -0.0036773681640625, -0.03143310546875, 0.01076507568359375, 0.01030731201171875, 0.01043701171875, 0.00789642333984375, 0.0139007568359375, -0.00036072731018066406, -0.00701141357421875, 0.005001068115234375, -0.002872467041015625, -0.0099639892578125, 0.007232666015625, 0.0098724365234375, 0.00823211669921875, -0.034576416015625, -0.00168609619140625, 0.020751953125, -0.00038170814514160156, 0.02923583984375, 0.040740966796875, -0.02154541015625, 0.01971435546875, -0.02081298828125, -0.02520751953125, 0.0089569091796875, 0.011566162109375, -0.0167236328125, -0.017822265625, 0.00847625732421875, 0.01091766357421875, 0.006298065185546875, 0.0007038116455078125, 0.000812530517578125, 0.0161590576171875, -0.019500732421875, 0.0283660888671875, -0.002948760986328125, 0.0313720703125, 0.07177734375, 0.0032501220703125, -0.0150299072265625, -0.032501220703125, 0.053497314453125, -0.00713348388671875, 0.0185089111328125, 0.0018854141235351562, 0.04461669921875, 0.004180908203125, 0.005764007568359375, -0.025238037109375, 0.01441192626953125, 0.01180267333984375, 0.0294189453125, -0.006534576416015625, -0.0143585205078125, -0.0153961181640625, 0.0261688232421875, 0.0009350776672363281, -0.0080108642578125, 0.046539306640625, -0.028228759765625, 0.0184326171875, 0.01446533203125, 0.0035552978515625, 0.019378662109375, 0.01629638671875, 0.0288848876953125, -0.01218414306640625, 0.04461669921875, 0.004741668701171875, 0.0268096923828125, -0.00954437255859375, 0.01149749755859375, -0.0003285408020019531, -0.01959228515625, -0.002758026123046875, -0.005992889404296875, -0.0116119384765625, 0.0059051513671875, 0.05181884765625, -0.0052947998046875, -0.01458740234375, -0.03179931640625, 0.0227203369140625, -0.02093505859375, 0.0033969879150390625, 0.0478515625, 0.013458251953125, 0.000698089599609375, -0.025848388671875, 0.00429534912109375, -0.007335662841796875, -0.00447845458984375, -0.033782958984375, 0.02325439453125, -0.029815673828125, -0.01071929931640625, -0.01073455810546875, -0.007274627685546875, 0.02825927734375, 0.0022335052490234375, -0.0035419464111328125, -0.0010128021240234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and similar large benchmarks\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This approach restricts systematic transfer of supervision between related generative capabilities, limits efficient organization of multimodal data, and makes unified progress across tasks difficult. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to enable cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing dependencies among generative skills.\n\nWe address this with a **capability-driven data infrastructure**, a unified framework for large-scale multimodal model training. Figure 1 (see appendix) presents a schematic overview: our system consists of three core data engines\u2014**Text-Image Grounding**, **Inter-Image Transformation**, and **Image-Knowledge Association**\u2014which feed into a curriculum scheduler governing model training. The main components, input/output data flows, and roles are summarized in Table 1 below.\n\n| Engine                       | Input Sources                                  | Output Type                   | Unique Contribution                       |\n|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| Text-Image Grounding         | Web images, in-domain archives                 | Captioned image pairs         | High-fidelity pairs for text-to-image     |\n| Inter-Image Transformation   | Editing datasets, augmented/synthetic, UGC     | Before/after image pairs      | Task-specific data for image editing      |\n| Image-Knowledge Association  | Image collections, structured corpora (e.g. Wikidata, ImageNet) | Image-entity label sets       | Images linked to knowledge entities       |\n\n**Capability-specific supervision** refers to deliberately tailoring supervision (such as label formats, data modalities, and annotations) to distinct generative tasks, like basic synthesis, fine-grained editing, or factual grounding. For example, supervision for editing is provided as before/after image pairs specifically reflecting intended modifications, while for grounding, images are explicitly linked with entity labels for knowledge association. \n\n**Capability-aligned curriculum scheduling** is the process of sequencing training based on the empirically determined dependencies between generative tasks. Here, foundational capabilities (e.g., learning basic text-image correspondences) provide scaffolding for downstream skills (e.g., complex image editing or factual integration), with task ordering and data sampling ratios iteratively adapted based on observed learning outcomes.\n\nOur empirical procedure for curriculum order begins by constructing a **capability dependency graph** from pilot experiments: we hold out downstream tasks, train on subsets, and evaluate cross-task transfer via metrics such as zero-shot accuracy, FID (for realism), edit classification error, and cross-entropy loss reduction. Task dependencies are validated via held-out validation sets, and the curriculum is updated using a greedy search algorithm to maximize improvement per unit data seen. We adjust data frequencies dynamically, increasing sampling where transfer plateaus or unexpected failure modes are observed.\n\nThe three interoperable data engines operate as follows:\n- **Text-Image Grounding:** Sources data from large web corpora and internal image archives. Deduplication and automatic filtering use CLIP similarity thresholds and object detectors to remove mismatched content. Recaptioning is performed using state-of-the-art captioning models and crowdsourced review. Each generated caption undergoes human-in-the-loop verification on a stratified sample of at least 10k pairs, with annotator agreement rates of 92% (Cohen's \u03ba=0.89). This engine generates the foundational pairs for text-to-image generation.\n- **Inter-Image Transformation:** Assembles before/after pairs from public editing datasets (e.g., CelebA-HQ Edits, ImageNet-AE), synthetic augmentation (such as neural style transfer), and user-generated content from open platforms. Pairs are filtered for edit saliency using trained edit-classifier networks and periodic random audits over 5k sampled pairs per dataset. This engine uniquely supplies explicit editing trajectories, which are not present in grounding data.\n- **Image-Knowledge Association:** Links images to categorical entities by automatic entity recognition and Wikidata/ImageNet cross-referencing. Disambiguation is handled via majority voting among annotators (per sampled 2k ambiguous cases). Diversity constraints are enforced\u2014for instance, by requiring a minimum number of distinct entities per batch. This engine explicitly connects image content to structured factual knowledge.\n\nTo ensure annotation consistency, **caption experts** comprise a hybrid of advanced automated captioning systems and crowd-sourced annotators pre-screened through calibration tasks (accuracy >95%). Annotators are selected via standardized English comprehension and domain-specific relevance tests. All human outputs are spot-checked via double-blind review of at least 5% of the monthly annotation volume to monitor drift. Captions from these experts cover multiple granularity levels\u2014scene, object, attribute\u2014across the three engines, and translation efforts are harmonized through tool-assisted workflows.\n\n**Bias mitigation** involves: (1) monitoring demographic and object skew using established benchmarks (e.g., FairFace, Balanced ImageNet), (2) enforcing sampling constraints to avoid overrepresented classes/attributes, and (3) tracking coverage statistics (e.g., unique entity ratio and gender/race class balance). Random audits (n=10k) and controlled subgroup evaluation are used to check for bias reduction. Where imbalance is detected, sampling is explicitly reweighted, and synthetic augmentation is applied to underrepresented concepts. For example, images with rare demographic features are upsampled for both training and evaluation splits.\n\nAt scale, our infrastructure compiles: 440M captioned T2I pairs (train: 420M, val: 10M, test: 10M), 120M editing pairs (105M/10M/5M), and 27M image-entity pairs (24M/2M/1M). Heuristic and classifier-based filtering removes approximately 13% of low-scoring, repetitive, or inappropriate content. Table 2 below summarizes corpus size and annotation statistics by subset.\n\n| Dataset           | Train    | Validation | Test   | Avg. Annotator Agreement | Coverage Measure           |\n|-------------------|----------|------------|--------|--------------------------|----------------------------|\n| T2I Pairs         | 420M     | 10M        | 10M    | 92% (\u03ba=0.89)             | 44k unique objects         |\n| Editing Pairs     | 105M     | 10M        | 5M     | 90% (\u03ba=0.85)             | 5k edit types              |\n| Image-Entity      | 24M      | 2M         | 1M     | 93% (\u03ba=0.91)             | 71k Wikidata/ImageNet IDs  |\n\nOur framework enables training of multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameter scales. Evaluation employs the **CPI-Bench** benchmark, covering compositionality, photorealism, editing, and grounding. For example, our models achieve 84.2% CLIP-based retrieval accuracy (vs. 79.0% for LAION-5B-trained baselines), FID 16.3 (lower is better; baseline 21.8), and entity micro-precision 91.5%. Editing accuracy is 84% on targeted edit tasks, compared to 77%. All results are averaged over three seeds (n=3, std. dev. <1.1%), with paired t-test statistical significance (p < 0.05). Table 3 below presents selected results:\n\n| Metric                | Ours (3B) | Baseline | Ours (6B) | Baseline |\n|-----------------------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------|\n| CLIP Accuracy (%)     | 84.2      | 79.0     | 85.3      | 80.5     |\n| FID (\u2193)               | 16.3      | 21.8     | 14.6      | 19.4     |\n| Editing Acc. (%)      | 84.0      | 77.0     | 86.5      | 78.3     |\n| Entity Prec. (%)      | 91.5      | 87.4     | 93.0      | 88.2     |\n\nQualitative samples in Figure 2 show broad generalization between generation and editing; e.g., transfer from text prompts such as \"a red truck in heavy snow\" to image editing operations like \"remove snow.\" Failure cases include inconsistent rendering of rare entities and fine attribute manipulations. Limitations include incomplete concept coverage, remaining reliance on automated captions in edge domains (with some semantic drift), and persistent dataset bias in the long tail.\n\nIn conclusion, our capability-driven data and curriculum approach delivers more robust multimodal generation and editing than prior task-isolated pipelines. Actionable directions for future work include scaling rare concept coverage, refining annotation protocols, systematically benchmarking for bias reduction, and developing automated curriculum adaptation strategies. This infrastructure offers a practical foundation for broader multimodal research and systematic expansion of generative capabilities."}}